# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification" (HoareLogic Exercises.pdf), problem 5 b), with its solution sheet.
id: multiply-by-adding
title: Multiplication by repeated addition
topic: Loops
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 5 b
tags: while, invariant, bounds

task:
  Give a proof outline and a loop invariant. The variables $x'$ and $y'$
  are logical variables: they keep the initial values of $x$ and $y$.

given:
  Loop condition B: $a <= y$

pre: x = x' && y = y' && y >= 0
post: z = x * y

program:
  {P}
  {R4}
  z := 0;            // i1
  {R3}
  a := 1;            // i2
  {I}
  while (a <= y) do {
    {I && a <= y}
    {R2}
    z := z + x;      // b1
    {R1}
    a := a + 1       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(a <= y)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  The counter $a$ starts at $1$, and each iteration adds one copy of $x$ to
  $z$. So after $a - 1$ iterations, $z$ is $x * (a - 1)$. The loop stops
  when $a$ reaches $y + 1$, which gives the bounds of $a$.
set: I = z = x * (a - 1) && 1 <= a <= y + 1
text:
  The loop rule with consequence has three premises, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $a <= y$: initialisation, preservation of $I$ by the
  body, and conclusion $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: R3, the weakest precondition of a := 1 for I
part: Initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The loop comes after two assignments, so initialisation is the triple
  $\{P\}$ `z := 0; a := 1` $\{I\}$. We calculate the weakest precondition
  of these two statements for $I$, from the last one. We substitute the
  assigned variable ($a$) with the assignment expression ($1$) in $I$.
calc:
  wp(a := 1, I)
  = (z = x * (a - 1) && 1 <= a <= y + 1)[a <- 1]     // I
  = z = x * (1 - 1) && 1 <= 1 <= y + 1     // a is replaced by 1
  = z = 0 && 1 <= 1 <= y + 1               // x * (1 - 1) is x * 0, which is 0
  = z = 0 && 0 <= y                        // 1 <= 1 is true; subtract 1 from both sides of 1 <= y + 1
set: R3 = z = 0 && 0 <= y

step: R4, the weakest precondition of z := 0 for R3
part: Initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute $z$ with $0$ in $R3$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(z := 0, R3)
  = (z = 0 && 0 <= y)[z <- 0]     // R3
  = 0 = 0 && 0 <= y      // z is replaced by 0
  = 0 <= y               // 0 = 0 is true, and true && A is A
set: R4 = 0 <= y

step: P implies R4
part: Initialisation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: P, R4
text:
  $R4$ is the weakest precondition of the two assignments for $I$. By the
  consequence rule, initialisation holds when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R4$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R4»
vc: x = x' && y = y' && y >= 0 ==> 0 <= y
why:
  $y >= 0$ is part of $P$.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of a := a + 1 for I
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  One iteration must keep the invariant:
  >>> {«I» && «a <= y : B»} z := z + x; a := a + 1 {«I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $a$ with $a + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(a := a + 1, I)
  = (z = x * (a - 1) && 1 <= a <= y + 1)[a <- a + 1]     // I
  = z = x * (a + 1 - 1) && 1 <= a + 1 <= y + 1    // a is replaced by a + 1
  = z = x * a && 1 <= a + 1 <= y + 1              // a + 1 - 1 is a
  = z = x * a && 0 <= a <= y                      // subtract 1 from each part of the bounds
set: R1 = z = x * a && 0 <= a <= y

step: R2, the weakest precondition of z := z + x for R1
part: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $z$ with $z + x$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(z := z + x, R1)
  = (z = x * a && 0 <= a <= y)[z <- z + x]     // R1
  = z + x = x * a && 0 <= a <= y          // z is replaced by z + x
  = z = x * a - x && 0 <= a <= y          // subtract x from both sides
  = z = x * (a - 1) && 0 <= a <= y        // x * a - x is x * (a - 1)
set: R2 = z = x * (a - 1) && 0 <= a <= y

step: I and B imply R2
part: Preservation
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && a <= y, R2
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «I» && «a <= y : B» ==> «R2»
vc: z = x * (a - 1) && 1 <= a <= y + 1 && a <= y ==> z = x * (a - 1) && 0 <= a <= y
why:
  $z = x * (a - 1)$ is part of $I$. From $1 <= a$ we get $0 <= a$, and
  $a <= y$ is $B$.

step: I and not B imply Q
part: Conclusion
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: I && !(a <= y), Q
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply the
  postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «I» && «!(a <= y) : ¬B» ==> «Q»
vc: z = x * (a - 1) && 1 <= a <= y + 1 && !(a <= y) ==> z = x * y
why:
  $!(a <= y)$ is $a > y$, that is $a >= y + 1$. Together with
  $a <= y + 1$ it gives $a = y + 1$, so $z = x * (y + 1 - 1) = x * y$.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The loop also terminates: $y + 1 - a$ decreases by one in every
  iteration, and it is positive whenever the body runs, since then
  $a <= y$. So $y + 1 - a$ is a variant.
